Q: What are good ways to improve a simple React user administration app? A: <p>This is a great project to learn and expand on your React knowledge and you've got a lot of the basics down but understanding the principles of modern React and learning best practices will help you take it to the next level.</p>
<p>You're trying to manage the users state in <code>service.js</code> but Service classes are meant to be stateless and should really be used to perform API calls. The <code>users</code> state is actually only being persisted in <code>App.jsx</code> where you have <strong><a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useState" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>useState</code></a></strong> .  There are few different ways you can go about improving your app but it really depends on your React knowledge, final application complexity, and preference.</p>
<p>I've put together some examples of different paths you can take to improve the current app and use that foundation to expand on:</p>
<hr />
<h2>Option 1 - Manage state in App.jsx (Basic/Recommended)</h2>
<p>Unless you plan to fetch users from an API, you could actually just move your User CRUD functions (<code>createUser</code>, <code>updateUser</code>, etc) into <code>App.jsx</code></p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>function App() {
    const [users, setUsers] = useState([
        {
            id: uid(),
            name: 'Max Mustermann',
            role: 1
        }
    ]);

    const createUser = (user) =&gt; {
        user.id = uid();

        setUsers((prevState) =&gt; ([
            ...prevState,
            user,
        ]));
    };

    const updateUser = (user) =&gt;
        setUsers((prevState) =&gt; {
            const index = prevState.findIndex(t =&gt; t.id === user.id)
            prevState[index] = user

            return prevState
        });


    const deleteUser = (user) =&gt;
        setUsers((prevState) =&gt; 
            prevState.filter((t) =&gt; t.id !== user.id));

    // Rest of file...
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Option 2 - <a href="https://react.dev/learn/reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks#extracting-your-own-custom-hook-from-a-component" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Custom React Hook</a> (Intermediate)</h2>
<p>Or to avoid nested prop drilling or if you expect your app to grow convert it into a custom hook to use throughout your app instead of passing down props from <code>App.jsx</code> :</p>
<p><code>service.js</code> -&gt; <code>useService.js</code> :</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { uid } from 'uid';

const initialUsers = [
    { 
        id: uid(),
        name: 'Max Mustermann',
        role: 1
    }
];

const useService = () =&gt; {
    const [users, setUsers] = useState(initialUsers);

    const getUserIndex = (id) =&gt; users.find((t) =&gt; t.id === id);

    const createUser = (user) =&gt;
        setUsers((prevState) =&gt; 
            prevState.concat({
                ...user, 
                id: uid(), // Could replace uid() with built-n React hook useId()
            })
        );

    const updateUser = (user) =&gt; 
        setUsers((prevState) =&gt; 
            prevState.with(getUserIndex(user.id), user));

    const deleteUser = (user) =&gt;
        setUsers((prevState) =&gt; 
            prevState.filter((t) =&gt; t.id !== user.id);

    return {
        users,
        createUser,
        updateUser,
        deleteUser,
    }
}

export default useService;
</code></pre>
<p>If you do plan to fetch the users via an API, you would add this method to your <code>service.js</code> :</p>
<pre><code>const fetchUsers = async () =&gt; {
    const response = await fetch(&quot;https://api.example.com&quot;);
    const data = await response.json();

    return data;
}
</code></pre>
<p>and call it in a <strong><a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>useEffect</code></a></strong>  <code>App.jsx</code>  ( )</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>function App() {
    const [users, setUsers] = useState([   
        {
            id: uid(),
            name: 'Max Mustermann',
            role: 1
        }
    ]);

    useEffect(() =&gt; {
        fetchUsers()
            .then((data) =&gt; setUsers(data))
    }, []) // Empty dependency array so it only fetches once

    // Rest of file...
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then you can access the state from anywhere in your app:</p>
<p><code>Users.jsx</code> :</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>/* eslint-disable react/prop-types */

import { User } from &quot;./User&quot;

import useService from &quot;./useService&quot;;

export const Users = () =&gt; {
    const { users } = useService();

    return (
        &lt;table className=&quot;table&quot;&gt;
            &lt;thead&gt;
                &lt;tr&gt;
                    &lt;th&gt;#&lt;/th&gt;
                    &lt;th&gt;Username&lt;/th&gt;
                    &lt;th&gt;Role&lt;/th&gt;
                    &lt;th&gt;Actions&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;/tr&gt;
            &lt;/thead&gt;

            &lt;tbody className=&quot;users&quot;&gt;
                {users.map(user =&gt; (
                    &lt;User key={user.id} user={user} /&gt;
                ))}
            &lt;/tbody&gt;
        &lt;/table&gt;       
    )
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>User.jsx</code> :</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>import useService = from &quot;./useService&quot;;

/* eslint-disable react/prop-types */
export const User = ({ user }) =&gt; {
    const { deleteUser, updateUser } = useService();

    const handleDelete = () =&gt; deleteUser(user);

    // Noticed you hadn't added your update logic but it could be something like this
    const handleUpdate = (payload) =&gt; updateUser({
        ...user,
        ...payload,
    });

    return (
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;{user.id}&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;span className=&quot;me-2&quot;&gt;{user.name}&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;select
                    className=&quot;form-control form-control-sm&quot;
                    name=&quot;roles&quot;
                    id=&quot;roles&quot;
                    value={user.role} 
                    disabled
                &gt;
                    &lt;option value=&quot;1&quot;&gt;Admin&lt;/option&gt;
                    &lt;option value=&quot;2&quot;&gt;User&lt;/option&gt;
                    &lt;option value=&quot;3&quot;&gt;Generic&lt;/option&gt;
                &lt;/select&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;button 
                    className=&quot;btn btn-outline-primary me-2&quot; 
                    onClick={handleUpdate}
                &gt;
                      &lt;i className=&quot;bi-pencil&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
                &lt;/button&gt;
                &lt;button className=&quot;btn btn-outline-danger&quot; onClick={handleDelete}&gt;
                    &lt;i className=&quot;bi-trash&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
                &lt;/button&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
    )
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Option 3 - <a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useReducer" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>useReducer</code></a> (Advanced)</h2>
<p>Or if you expect your state management to grow, you can create a reducer for your user state updates and others for other actions:</p>
<p><code>reducer.js</code> :</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { uid } from 'uid';
import { useReducer } from 'react';

const initialState = [
    { 
        id: uid(),
        name: 'Max Mustermann',
        role: 1
    }
];

const reducer = (prevState, action) =&gt; {
    const getUserIndex = (id) =&gt; prevState.find((t) =&gt; t.id === id);

    switch(action.type) {
        case &quot;CREATE_USER&quot;:
            const user = action.payload;

            user.id = uid();

            return prevState.with(-1, user); // New state with created user 
        case &quot;UPDATE_USER&quot;:
            const { id } = action.payload;

            prevState.splice(getUserIndex(id), 1, action.payload); 

            return prevState; // New state with updated user
        case &quot;DELETE_USER&quot;:
            const { id } = action.payload;

            prevState.splice(getUserIndex(id), 1); 

            return prevState; // New state without deleted user

        default:
            return state;
    }
};

// Export reducer to use with React hook
export const usersReducer = () =&gt; useReducer(reducer, initialState);
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Here are some other React references you might find useful later on in your project:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><a href="https://react.dev/learn/managing-state" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Managing State</a></p>
</li>
<li><p><strong><a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useContext" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>useContext</code></a></strong></p>
</li>
<li><p><strong><a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useRef" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>useRef</code></a></strong></p>
</li>
<li><p><strong><a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useId" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>useId</code></a></strong></p>
</li>
</ul>

Q: How do I use async await in a regular for loop A: <p><code>await</code> works perfectly fine inside a regular <code>for</code> loop.</p>
<p>Your issue is not the loop itself. The problem is that you are calling <code>save()</code> on every iteration. You only need to modify the document in memory during the loop and save it once at the end.</p>
<p>Also, declare <code>index</code> with <code>let</code>, otherwise it becomes a global variable.</p>
<pre><code>async function addBot(game, numOfBots) {
  for (let index = 0; index &lt; numOfBots; index++) {
    game.players.push({ nickName: &quot;Bot&quot; });
  }

  await game.save();
  return game;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usage:</p>
<pre><code>await addBot(game, 3);
</code></pre>
<p>A few notes:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>A normal <code>for</code> loop is fine with <code>async/await</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>await</code> inside that loop runs sequentially.</p>
</li>
<li><p>In this case, saving once is better than saving on every iteration.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>If this still only stores one bot, then the problem is likely in your Mongoose schema for <code>players</code>, not in <code>async/await</code>.</p>
